    "title": "Los Angeles Dodgers vs. Cleveland Guardians: How to watch Shohei Ohtani's first pitching start tonight, streaming info and more",
    "text": "Shohei Ohtani and the Los Angeles Dodgers play the Cleveland Guardians tonight in L.A., here's how to watch. (Brandon Sloter/IOS/Getty Images) (Brandon Sloter via Getty Images)Baseball is officially in full swing as we enter the second week of the MLB season, and one of the biggest stars of the league. Shohei Ohtani, is scheduled to pitch for the Los Angeles Dodgers tonight when the team hosts the Cleveland Guardians at Dodger Stadium. The game is the second in the Dodgers-Guardians series; the Guardians won the first game of the series on Monday, handing the Dodgers their first loss of the season. Tanner Bibee will pitch for Cleveland tonight.You can watch tonight's Dodgers vs. Guardians game on SportsNet LA and Guardians.TV, as well as on MLB.TV. Here's what you need to know so you can watch Shohei Ohtani's first pitching start of the season.AdvertisementDate: March 31Times: 10:10 p.m. ET/7:10 p.m. PTLocation: Dodger Stadium, Los AngelesChannels: SportsNet LA, Guardians.TVStreaming: MLB.TVWhen is Shohei Ohtani pitching?Shohei Ohtani is scheduled to take the mound for the Los Angeles Dodgers for the first time this season tonight, Tuesday, March 31, at 10:10 p.m. ET/7:10 p.m. PT against the Cleveland Guardians.What channels will the Dodgers vs. Guardians game be on?The Dodgers vs. Guardians game will be broadcast on SportsNet LA and Guardians.TV. If you're out of market, you can catch the game on MLB.TV.AdvertisementHow to watch the Dodgers vs. Guardians without cableThe Dodgers vs. Guardians game is available on regional sports networks SportsNet LA and Guardians.TV which are also available on DirecTV and YouTube TV, or if you're out of market, you can watch on MLB.TV.An MLB.TV subscription is a great way to catch all your favorite teams' out-of-market games live. New MLB.TV subscriptions are available through the ESPN App. New subscribers who purchase MLB.TV must do so through the ESPN App and are required to sign up for a 1-month free trial of ESPN Unlimited in order to sign up for MLB.TV. After 1 month, ESPN auto-renews at $29.99/month, or you can cancel the trial at any time. ESPN is not required to maintain an MLB.TV subscription. Existing ESPN Unlimited subscribers have the opportunity to add an MLB.TV subscription in 2026 for $134.99 per season, down from $149.99. Monthly subscription options are also available for $29.99, with new subscribers eligible for a free month of ESPN Unlimited.",
